. *********************************
. *********************************
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1
. 
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         * We assume that the causal effect of having a daughter is identified
>  in a sample of parents of a single child without further adjustment (see PAP
> )
.         * !! NOTE THIS IS THE AVERAGE CAUSAL EFFECT IN THE POPULATION OF PARE
> NTS OF A SINGLE CHILD !!
.         * We further assume that agreement to statements "Women should rather
>  care about family than career" [plh0301] and "Men should involve in housewor
> k" [plh0303] 
.         * are good measures of support for gender equality (hypothesis 1): 
.         * Less agreement to the former and more agreement to the latter is mo
> re likely to indicate support for gender equality.
.         * Accordingly, we run a seemingly unrelated ordered probit regression
>  analysis with famvscareer [plh0301] and menhousework [plh0303] as dependent 
> variables
.         * and the dummy variable daughter as independent variable in a sample
>  of respondents with a single child (parity2!=1)
.         * We apply weights cross-sectionally calibrated weights (phrf)
.         * To account for non-independence of observatons, we cluster standard
>  errors at the household level
.         * The null we test against is that the effect of having daughters is 
> <=0 on both outcomes
.         * I.e., we have to consider a one-sided test adjusted for multiple te
> sting
.         * Since the used method provides a joint confidence band, this is equ
> al to assessing whether there is at least 1 significant effect
.         * We report all coefficients and statistics but only export the resul
> t with the smallest p-value to comply to the provided template
.         * !! NOTE THAT AT AN A PRIORI CONFIDENCE LEVEL OF 0.05 WITHOUT PREDER
> MINED SIGN OF THE EFFECT, THE CONFIDENCE LEVEL BECOMES 0.025
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         * The below code follows the conception outlined in the PAP
.         * There are two "technical" deviations from the preliminary code also
>  provided in the PAP:
.         * suest takes iweights rather than pweights
.         * the sample restriction is already implemented in the data preparati
> on and therefore omitted for redundancy
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
. 
. 
